    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **James Wood** (75.97) stands as our premier offensive anchor. Facing a soft matchup against the left-handed Bratt (5.90 xERA), **James Wood** benefits from an elite xwOBA profile and a prime leadoff spot, making him our strongest play despite the lack of a platoon advantage. **Brice Turang** (72.70) and **Austin Riley** (72.41) follow close behind; **Brice Turang** exploits a massive +19% SP skill edge against Feltner, while **Austin Riley** is buoyed by a +16% park factor boost, suggesting that even in the bottom half of the order, he is positioned to capitalize on a favorable environment.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision today involved **Isaac Paredes** (52.31), who narrowly missed the starting cut. While **Isaac Paredes** offers a solid floor, the optimizer prioritized the specific situational utility of **Josh Naylor** (52.93) and **Gabriel Moreno** (51.75). The latter was chosen for his higher frequency in a #3 spot, a leverage point that narrowly eclipsed the upside **Isaac Paredes** would have provided against the tougher Burke. We are also monitoring the risk associated with **Corbin Carroll** (65.34); while his speed remains vital, he faces a daunting task against Griffin. However, a \"Slow SP Delivery\" tag at the mound gives **Corbin Carroll** a calculated 100% success probability for stolen base attempts, forcing us to start him despite the defensive-minded matchup. We remain cautious with **Edwin Arroyo** (44.91) and **Jose Caballero** (51.18) today, as both are anchored by bottom-of-the-order assignments and tough opposing xERAs, requiring them to rely heavily on their speed profiles to avoid the sub-40 \"Below Floor\" threshold.",
